Several refinements to the SKE Resource Utilization HTML report:
1. Layout — per-namespace cards, 2 per row, scrollable. Replace the single combined bar chart at the top with individual per-namespace cards arranged 2 per row in a scrollable grid (this needs to comfortably scale to 15–20 namespaces, not just the current 6). Each card shows that namespace’s own CPU and Memory data.
2. Replace the bar chart with pill-shaped progress bars. Build these as plain HTML/CSS (not Plotly) for precise control over the shape: a fully-rounded track bar in a light gray tone matching the new background, with an inner green fill bar whose width equals the current-utilization percentage against that namespace’s quota limit. One pill for CPU, one for Memory, per card. Keep the existing hover-to-reveal-per-deployment-breakdown behavior, implemented as a lightweight CSS-only tooltip (:hover + positioned pseudo-element) — no external JS library, to stay consistent with the fully self-contained/no-CDN requirement already used for the Plotly charts.
3. Light theme. Switch the report’s overall background to a pale/light blue, with dark, high-contrast text. Update the Plotly pie charts’ paper_bgcolor/plot_bgcolor/font colors to match — they currently still render against a dark background and need to match the new theme.
4. Fix the pie chart math — this is a correctness fix, not just styling. Currently each pie’s 100% represents the sum of only the currently-active namespaces’ usage, which is misleading — it implies full utilization when it isn’t. Change it so 100% represents the tenant’s total CPU quota (and separately, total Memory quota) — the sum of every namespace’s quota limit, including namespaces with zero current usage. Each namespace’s slice = its current usage ÷ that true total. Add one additional “Unused / Available” slice (styled in a distinct muted color) representing the quota that isn’t currently being consumed by any namespace. This should use data the script already collects — just change the denominator and add the remainder slice.
5. Simplify namespace display names — as a general, reusable rule, not a hardcoded list (this needs to keep working as more namespaces get added). Strip the common tenant prefix (e.g., t-55042-clmt-clmt-) from each namespace name, then format the remaining suffix into a friendly label using a small token-lookup table (e.g., int→”Integration”, mig→”Migration”, sit→”SIT”, orch→”Orchestration”, dev→”Dev”), splitting on hyphens and capitalizing each token, with trailing numbers kept attached to their word (dev01→”Dev 01”). Compound names must stay distinguishable: int-dev01 should become “Integration Dev 01”, clearly different from a plain dev01 → “Dev 01”, since these represent genuinely different environments despite both containing “dev01”. Keep the original raw namespace name available as a small muted subtitle or hover tooltip on each card, for anyone who needs the exact technical name.
